 #edb/ref 17592186345113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119925 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119925 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119925 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Dependence and Independence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120052 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ec28c6e-377f-591a-9037-3511702a70c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120147 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-3+1 \\\\ -2-0+2 \\\\ 4-0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62e09db0-cf5e-5972-a5d2-6baa771c45b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120148 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1061 41352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120877 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1061 41353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120878 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1061 41354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120879 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1061 41357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120880 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1061 41358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1061 41359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1061 41362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1065 6811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121122 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1064 "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1065 6812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121123 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n![](images/bd/bd4df860628c44295dfd621e6b0e64839c2657c2baa094def656ea8b90ece9c8.svg)\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n![](images/50/5048e293fd6bb60a0ca95451aebd3bb77d0f748bb54e9f5dd044f60fb34c28ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation\n\n![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1065 6813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121124 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1064 "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1065 6814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121125 #edb/ref 1067 "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238185 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f6b3754-c8d5-5b37-8d18-ef83f5e9749c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238202 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11a7491d-2ce9-57b5-afc5-475cffb86e52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238231 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1564190f-a603-5a1b-99ee-d29458e3bdf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238401 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238401 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238401 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238525 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "366bc6b5-4bab-5ba8-8018-c4bc7223b115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238638 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238638 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238638 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "430d277a-8ae9-5f5c-b444-1e030f7d4b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238953 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238953 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238953 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67430bf0-add9-5760-a0d2-5687fc3ea820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238970 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238970 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238970 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69582467-05df-57a2-b597-530abbc3877d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239082 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239082 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239082 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "771afedc-1ab9-538b-9c8d-99514ab7bd87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239178 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239178 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239178 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81792f0f-eb2e-5c8b-9e65-a32b2c8a647e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239208 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84b00443-b844-5946-b246-e24069d4f374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239333 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93d981b6-c7f1-52e4-a22e-60dd625357db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239468 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239468 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239468 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a297bb83-555e-542b-b729-eb9040d9fd2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239628