 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240715 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "daefdae9-19ab-5066-96c6-64e5270f1794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e5fd550-1965-59ea-b83f-2c578a1e0197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241027 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "070a6728-701f-5a05-b9c9-03b5e1530505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241028 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c05cb83d-c779-50b1-a772-7d4551eea239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241031 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af32236d-520e-5b71-b7ec-9663a181498c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241033 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 15 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -7 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272432 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04fd0e5f-aec1-5e47-9884-6914dcdde436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272442 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272442 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272442 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05d82b46-aff2-515f-9d9e-763c1d8d6d4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272530 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "100da246-a4f5-543a-91d0-e6d3a1f36af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272932 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272932 #ed